#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f585bd05-7dac-55b1-a0ab-f8320242a5df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis of a Span Given Some Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "683556db-c65d-5279-ac14-7dfaa496db0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5584" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose that $V = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf a_3\\}$. Given that $\\mathcal B = \\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf b\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V, \\mathbf{a}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{a}_2$, and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{c}{a}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= a_{3} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{3} - proj_{v_{2}}a_{3} \\\\\n&= a_{3} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{2}}{v_{2} \\cdot v_{2}}v_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - (-\\frac{2}{4})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{0}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - 0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\n$$\nc=-\\dfrac 3 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)} = 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133527 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9870ad37-3e54-5403-936c-09a321d4a0cf" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis Using the Gram-Schmidt Process"}] :question/id #uuid "87d88110-e10d-553c-983d-6c22d3f19895" :question/math-academy-id "e-5585" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose that $V = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf a_3\\}$. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$, and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}$?" :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{2} &= a_{2} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{2} \\\\\n&= a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= a_{3} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{3} - proj_{v_{2}}a_{3} \\\\\n&= a_{3} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{2}}{v_{2} \\cdot v_{2}}v_{2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} - (-\\frac{3}{3})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{3}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + 2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac 3 2\n$$\n\n$$\nc=-\\dfrac 3 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}=-1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133600 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866ab12b-7f51-55f8-9e7b-e39f9c3b4b44" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix Given Some Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/id #uuid "06f90a75-b288-5975-9e74-994c180be4d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8321" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\textrm{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3$ of the matrix $A$, and an **orthonormal** basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{a}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{a}_2$, and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\mid \\,d\\, \\mid$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= a_{3} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{3} - proj_{v_{2}}a_{3} \\\\\n&= a_{3} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{2}}{v_{2} \\cdot v_{2}}v_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{10}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{20}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_3$ dividing it by\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf{v}_3\\| = \\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{3} &= \\frac{v_{3}}{‖v_{3}‖} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}\\,d\\, & = \\left\\end{vmatrix}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "866ab12b-7f51-55f8-9e7b-e39f9c3b4b44" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix Given Some Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84b3f2b3-3533-5632-8d48-33b5a03a794a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131871} {:answer/id #uuid "8cad4b74-4df6-5bad-a427-39e418f23532" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131872} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7469b0-2bdd-5412-ab4a-a07712885fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131873} {:answer/id #uuid "03c7ad68-63ac-5